  "contentData": [
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "3ef5c851-77d2-46af-9a22-e2bfadd01829",
      "udi": "umb://element/1a344dfe2a934d8caf401eff39e1d044",
      "title": "What Are Geotechnical Investigations?",
      "content": {
        "markup": "<p><strong>This comprehensive guide outlines key testing for common geotechnical services.</strong></p>\n<p>From field investigations to laboratory analysis, these exact procedures ensure that major road expansions of the N3 and N2 as well as new hospitals in Burgersfort are designed with a thorough understanding of the ground’s capabilities and limitations.</p>\n<p><strong><br>Geotechnical services are critical to ensuring the success and safety of construction work.&nbsp;</strong></p>\n<p>These services provide the foundational data needed to assess the suitability of soil, rock and underground conditions at a site, offering critical insights to inform design and planning phases.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>By conducting in-situ testing such as Dynamic Cone Penetrometer (DCP) test, engineers can gauge the load-bearing potential of the soil.</p>\n<p>These field evaluations are complemented by laboratory tests that classify soil types, measure permeability and assess strength under a range of likely and extreme conditions.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Triaxial and oedometer testing allow for a deeper understanding of soil deformation and settlement behavior, while specialized investigations in areas like dolomitic stability ensure that no potential risks—such as sinkholes—are overlooked.</p>",
        "blocks": {
          "contentData": [],
          "settingsData": []
        }
      },
      "images": [
        {
          "key": "17b57af5-3672-44eb-b7fe-d7fbbf9b854a",
          "mediaKey": "98fbcb54-8006-4dd0-bea6-613400f6308b"
        },
        {
          "key": "958352a1-b4ce-4d38-b81e-a32000a0a5b7",
          "mediaKey": "02d944c2-fa28-4016-aec4-4d163e016c07"
        }
      ],
      "anchorID": "01"
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "3ef5c851-77d2-46af-9a22-e2bfadd01829",
      "udi": "umb://element/78a67566128042f5824237bf1757ccd0",
      "title": "Centreline Investigations",
      "content": {
        "markup": "<p>Centreline investigations involve a series of tests and procedures to determine the characteristics and suitability of soil along a planned road alignment.&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>In-Situ Testing</strong>&nbsp;<br>Nuclear densities are performed to assess the compaction of the soil.</p>\n<p>Dynamic Cone Penetrometer (DCPS) tests provide a measure of the in-situ strength of the soil layers, giving insight into its load-bearing capacity.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Hand-dug test pits allow for direct observation and analysis of soil strata and conditions.&nbsp;<br><br><strong>Laboratory Testing</strong></p>\n<p>Soil Classifications involve a range of testing (including Atterberg Limits) that provides valuable information about the type of soil present on a site and it’s behaviour under different conditions.</p>\n<p>MDD (Maximum Dry Density, known as a MOD) testing establishes the maximum unit weight a soil sample can achieve at its optimum moisture content.</p>\n<p>The Road Indicator test evaluates the soil’s performance as a road material, including its susceptibility to shrinkage and swelling as well as providing a grading envelope for the material.</p>\n<p>CBR (California Bearing Ratio) are common tests used to evaluate subgrade strength, primarily of roads, pavements and foundations.</p>\n<p>UCS (Unconfined Compressive Strength) testing determines the maximum compressive stress that soil can bear while&nbsp;Indirect Tensile Strength (ITS) determines soil’s strength and adherence against fatigue, temperature cracking and rutting.</p>",
        "blocks": {
          "contentData": [],
          "settingsData": []
        }
      },
      "images": [
        {
          "key": "403cf0a1-625c-4b16-b410-f0119bc3b94f",
          "mediaKey": "534d3312-f5fd-4867-8c6e-a5e1662c7222"
        },
        {
          "key": "532d65f0-6c87-418f-91c3-fe4f37b2e347",
          "mediaKey": "5aa9f0fa-b920-4b03-805a-19062707cc33"
