        "settingsData": []
      }
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "3ef5c851-77d2-46af-9a22-e2bfadd01829",
      "udi": "umb://element/3434ec3081b5470b943c1ce0bfb7e7e8",
      "title": "In-Situ Investigations",
      "content": {
        "markup": "<p>In-situ investigations are the first step in understanding the soil and subsurface conditions of a construction site.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>These investigations involve testing the ground directly at the location where construction will take place, providing real-time data on soil strength, density, moisture content and collapse potential.</p>\n<p>The Collapse Potential Test assess the potential for soil to swell when wet or collapse when dry, influencing foundation stability.</p>\n<p>Key tests include in-situ Dynamic Cone Penetrometer (DCP) testing and Deep Probe Super Heavy (DPSH) testing for assessing in-situ soil bearing capacity, while hand-dug test pits allow for direct observation and profiling of soil layers on site.<br><br>When more thorough investigations are required, borehole drilling is performed to assess the underlying rock strata and obtain deeper and more revealing Shelby tube samples for further analysis.<br><br>Uniaxle Compressive Strength, Point Load Testing and Core Logging are also part of our specialised in-situ tests.<br><br>These services provide the foundational data needed to assess the suitability of soil, rock and underground conditions at a site, offering critical insights to inform design and planning phases.&nbsp;</p>",
        "blocks": {
          "contentData": [],
          "settingsData": []
        }
      },
      "images": [
        {
          "key": "696c4892-eca7-46a6-a8dc-8944ac04917a",
          "mediaKey": "eaced875-afe5-4824-b732-52061595d210"
        }
      ],
      "anchorID": "04"
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "3ef5c851-77d2-46af-9a22-e2bfadd01829",
      "udi": "umb://element/e62a713dd2fa4177a7d8d68fa7ad5cd6",
      "title": "Specialised Testing",
      "content": {
        "markup": "<p>These test  focus on how soils behave under sustained loading conditions.</p>\n<p>Single and Double Oedometer testing measures the soil’s ability to compress and consolidate under applied loads, helping predict potential settlement issues.</p>\n<p>Anisotropic Consolidation tests provide detailed data on soil behavior under varied stress directions, essential for complex load conditions.</p>\n<p>Swell Pressure and Volume Collapse Tests determine the potential for soils, especially clay-rich ones, to expand or collapse, which is crucial for construction in variable moisture environments.</p>\n<p>Free Swell and Collapse Potential Tests assess the potential for soil to swell when wet or collapse when dry, influencing foundation stability.</p>\n<p>Triaxial Testing (CD, CU, and UU) evaluate soil strength, deformation, and pore pressure under simulated field conditions. Multistage triaxial testing provides a detailed understanding of soil behavior under various stress levels.</p>\n<p>The Shearbox test measures the shear strength of soils by applying horizontal forces to a confined sample, crucial for slope stability and retaining wall design.</p>\n<p>Drained Settlement Density Determination testing assesses how much soil will settle and its final density under drained conditions, vital for foundation and load-bearing structure design, while Wax Density Testing determines soil density by coating the sample with wax and measuring its volume and mass.</p>\n<p>Permeability Tests (Constant Head, Falling Head, and Flexible Head Permeability) measure the ease with which water moves through the soil, crucial for drainage design and slope stability.</p>\n<p>A Pinhole Test is used to identify and classify the erodibility of the soil based on the flow rate of discharge water, cloudiness, the size of the hole, and the applied pressure.</p>\n<p>The Crumb Test assesses the durability of soil aggregates and their breakdown under mechanical agitation or exposure to water whereas the Lump Test evaluates the degree of saturation and void ratio of a dry sample to determine maximum saturation point.</p>",
        "blocks": {
          "contentData": [],
          "settingsData": []
        }
      },
      "images": [
        {
          "key": "bc74e7b2-5ecf-4908-89cd-f5e952af1526",
          "mediaKey": "e8756f72-4d63-4f4e-a93c-b52b2b33eed6"
        },
        {
          "key": "65733d96-f92a-4dd9-a9bf-d64cc2f36403",
          "mediaKey": "08c9bb39-31cf-490a-8a25-2d9e3c4bc45e"
        }
      ],
      "anchorID": "06"
    },
